**Stack vs heap.**

| | Stack | Heap |
|---|---|---|
| Used for | Local variables, parameters, return addresses | Dynamically allocated objects |
| Allocation | Automatic, by moving the stack pointer | Explicit (`malloc`/`new`), by the allocator |
| Lifetime | Until the function returns | Until freed |
| Speed | Very fast, cache-friendly | Slower, may fragment |
| Size | Small, fixed (often a few MB); overflow causes stack overflow | Large, limited by system memory |
| Management | Automatic | Programmer (or GC/RAII) |

**Memory leak.** Heap memory that was allocated but never freed and can no longer be reached, so the program's memory use grows until it slows down or crashes.

```c
void f(void) {
    int *p = malloc(100 * sizeof *p);
    if (error) return;   // leak: p is never freed
    free(p);
}
```

Common causes: early returns or exceptions skipping `free`, overwriting a pointer before freeing it, and forgetting to free in destructors. Prevention: free on every path, use RAII and smart pointers in C++, and keep clear ownership rules. Detection: Valgrind, AddressSanitizer (`-fsanitize=address`), and heap profilers.

**Declaring a dynamic array.**

```c
int n = 10;
int *arr = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int));   // C
if (!arr) { /* handle failure */ }
/* use arr[i] */
free(arr);
```

```cpp
int* arr = new int[n]();      // C++ raw
delete[] arr;

std::vector<int> v(n);        // preferred: automatic memory management, resizable
auto p = std::make_unique<int[]>(n);
```

A variable-length array (`int a[n];`) is allocated on the stack in C99 and is not standard C++, so a large `n` risks stack overflow. Dynamic arrays are fixed once allocated; to grow, `realloc` in C or use `std::vector`, which doubles capacity for amortized O(1) appends.
